<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Francesco Guicciardini</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1483">1483</a>–<a href="/years/1540">1540</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Francesco Guicciardini (1483–1540) was a Florentine historian, diplomat and statesman. He served as ambassador to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, as papal governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modena">Modena</a>, Reggio and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romagna">Romagna</a> and as an adviser to the first Medici duke, and he was a friend and critic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niccolo-machiavelli">Machiavelli</a>. His History of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, covering 1490 to 1534, is considered a founding work of modern historiography for its use of documents and its analysis of political motives.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/francesco-guicciardini.jpg" alt="Francesco Guicciardini" width="320" height="421" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cristofano dell'Altissimo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ritratto_di_Francesco_Guicciardini.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
